WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.800
A rod cell in your retina can register a single photon.

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.840
It reports that photon by switching a current off.

3
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.080
In the dark, the rod runs a steady inward current and releases transmitter without

4
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.920
pause. Light reduces both.

5
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.980
How can a fall carry a signal?

6
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:31.233
In the dark, the rod contains a messenger called cGMP.

7
00:00:31.853 --> 00:00:37.813
It binds directly to cation channels in the membrane, with no kinase in between, and

8
00:00:37.813 --> 00:00:39.093
holds some of them open.

9
00:00:40.393 --> 00:00:45.173
Sodium and calcium flow in through those channels, and that inflow is the dark

10
00:00:45.173 --> 00:00:50.993
current. Because of it, the rod sits partly depolarized and releases glutamate

11
00:00:50.993 --> 00:00:56.193
continuously. The rod must maintain this baseline all through the dark.

12
00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:03.697
Rhodopsin, the rod's receptor, holds a bent molecule called retinal.

13
00:01:04.577 --> 00:01:07.877
While retinal is bent, the receptor stays off.

14
00:01:08.897 --> 00:01:12.917
A photon straightens the retinal, and rhodopsin becomes active.

15
00:01:14.037 --> 00:01:19.917
Active rhodopsin switches on transducin, the rod's G protein, and transducin

16
00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:23.237
switches on a phosphodiesterase, PDE6.

17
00:01:23.237 --> 00:01:28.957
This enzyme destroys cGMP, a messenger that was already there.

18
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.960
So cGMP falls, and the channels close.

19
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.680
The inward current shrinks, and the membrane hyperpolarizes.

20
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.280
Glutamate release drops, and the next cell reads that drop.

21
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.940
In a toad rod, the dark current is roughly 20 picoamps.

22
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.580
Each rhodopsin that a photon turns dips that current by about one picoamp.

23
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.040
The nose works the other way round.

24
00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.680
Each sensory neuron in the nose has just one receptor, chosen from a very large

25
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:10.420
family. An odorant binds its receptor, and a G protein then switches on an enzyme,

26
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:17.480
adenylyl cyclase. The messenger cAMP rises, and it directly opens a cation channel

27
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:18.600
related to the rod's.

28
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.640
The neuron depolarizes and fires.

29
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:26.803
In the rod, light makes cGMP fall.

30
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:31.363
In the nose, a smell makes cAMP rise.

31
00:02:32.483 --> 00:02:38.783
In an artery wall, nitric oxide makes that same cGMP rise, and the muscle

32
00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:41.163
relaxes. Keep this rule.

33
00:02:41.943 --> 00:02:47.403
A signal is a change from a baseline, and a cell can read a change in either direction.
